paw 2 hits, say, <enter>?  You'd have to take into account the nonlocal 
correlations introduced by distant paws as well.  In addition, you have 
the fingers of the player that were still on the board.  All of this 
increases complexity and reduces long-time correlations.  In statistical 
mechanics, near-by collisions of particles are also correlated, still 
the assumption of molecular chaos works well.  I said already that a 
similar assumption is less reliable in the case at hand but that it will 
probably do (also it is the only one I would venture doing the analysis 
with:-).

